WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.767 --> 00:00:04.003
-Our first guest tonight
is an Academy Award-nominated,

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:06.239
Emmy-winning actor you know,
from movies like "Sideways"

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:09.075
and "Cinderella Man,"
as well as the show "Billions."

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:10.509
His podcast,

00:00:10.509 --> 00:00:11.944
Paul Giamatti's "Chinwag"
with Stephen Asma,

00:00:11.944 --> 00:00:13.013
is available on YouTube

00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:14.146
and wherever you listen
to podcasts.

00:00:14.146 --> 00:00:16.049
He also stars in
"The Holdovers,"

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:17.684
which is in theaters
now nationwide.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:18.885
Let's take a look.

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:20.653
-There was an incident
when I was at Harvard

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:23.556
with my roommate.

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:24.957
-And?

00:00:24.957 --> 00:00:27.660
-He accused me of copying
from his senior thesis.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.096
Plagiarizing. Ahh.

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:31.531
-Well, did you?
-No!

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:34.500
He stole from me.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.768
But that
blue-blooded prick's family

00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:37.203
had allies on the faculty.

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:39.505
I mean, their last name is
on a library, for Christ's sake,

00:00:39.505 --> 00:00:42.941
so he accused me in order
to sanitize his treachery,

00:00:42.941 --> 00:00:46.179
and they threw me out.

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:48.381
-So you got kicked out
of Harvard for cheating?

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:51.117
-No, I got kicked out
of Harvard for hitting him.

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.052
-You hit him?
What, like, punched him out?

00:00:53.052 --> 00:00:55.955
-No, I hit him with a car.

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.056
-Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:57.056 --> 00:00:58.791
Paul Giamatti, everybody!

00:00:58.791 --> 00:01:01.027
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:15.842 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:17.176
It's so lovely to have you
back here.

00:01:17.176 --> 00:01:18.243
-It's a real pleasure
to be back.

00:01:18.243 --> 00:01:19.445
Thank you, sir.

00:01:19.445 --> 00:01:20.813
-You were scheduled to be here
the first day

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:21.914
of the writer's strike.

00:01:21.914 --> 00:01:23.282
You were May 2nd,
which was the first day

00:01:23.282 --> 00:01:24.584
we canceled shows.

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:26.152
-And everything shut down.
-Everything shut down.

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:27.920
-Bad luck.
-Ol' Bad Luck Giamatti.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.422
-Ol' Bad Luck Giamatti.

00:01:29.422 --> 00:01:30.923
Bad penny.
-Yeah.

00:01:30.923 --> 00:01:32.392
-No, it was really --

00:01:32.392 --> 00:01:33.626
-Well, I'm glad.

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:35.261
The nice thing is,
we will talk about your podcast,

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:36.763
which we were going to do then,
but we're also,

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.031
we have this beautiful movie
to talk about.

00:01:38.031 --> 00:01:39.565
I love this so much.
-Thank you.

00:01:39.565 --> 00:01:41.801
And this is a movie,
it's called "The Holdovers."

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.303
And it's about the kids
who have to stay

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:45.238
at a private school
over the holiday break

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:46.372
because they don't have
anywhere else to go.

00:01:46.372 --> 00:01:48.841
-Nobody wants them, yep.
-You're a teacher.

00:01:48.841 --> 00:01:52.244
And this was actually
filmed at a school,

00:01:52.244 --> 00:01:54.346
the rival school
to where you actually went?

00:01:54.346 --> 00:01:55.815
-Which I'd forgotten.

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:58.751
We we filmed in five different
schools to create one school,

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:01.653
and one of them we shot at
was this Deerfield Academy

00:02:01.653 --> 00:02:03.789
in Massachusetts,
outside Boston.

00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:05.358
And I'd forgotten
it was the rival school

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:07.093
to where I went,
which was Choate.

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:08.928
-Right.
-So when I was there --

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:11.831
I had these kind of
horrible flashbacks

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:12.999
while I was there,

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:15.834
I was like, "Oh, my God,
I remember this place,

00:02:15.834 --> 00:02:17.003
going to swim meets,"

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:18.504
which, anybody who was
on a swimming team,

00:02:18.504 --> 00:02:20.306
it's the most miserable
sport in the world.

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:22.642
-It's the worst. Yes.
-It's the worst.

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:24.376
And you're in the bus
and it's winter

00:02:24.376 --> 00:02:26.979
and it's dark out and
you're leaving and you're wet

00:02:26.979 --> 00:02:29.115
and you're chafed
and you're like...

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:32.118
And you've had your ass kicked
in the backstroke event,

00:02:32.118 --> 00:02:33.385
and it's just horrible.

00:02:33.385 --> 00:02:34.854
-It's horrible!

00:02:34.854 --> 00:02:37.156
Also, the way there is even
worse, because it's cold out

00:02:37.156 --> 00:02:38.725
and you know,
you're about to get wet.

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:41.194
-That's right,
so it just is all bad.

00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:44.130
And that was this vivid,
vivid memory of that.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.099
-This is an interesting,
because it's a period piece,

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:48.534
but it's also a period
you were alive for.

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:50.303
It is the '70s.
-Yes.

00:02:50.303 --> 00:02:51.604
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:53.138
Thank you for
pointing that out!

00:02:53.138 --> 00:02:55.108
-I'm just saying, you played --

00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:56.609
-It was weird, because
people kept saying,

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:57.944
"Oh, it's a period film.

00:02:57.944 --> 00:03:00.145
I'm like, "That's not possible."

00:03:00.145 --> 00:03:02.715
I'm like, period is like,
horse and carriage

00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:05.017
and bows and arrows and stuff.

00:03:05.017 --> 00:03:08.921
It's not the pipes and
smoking in the movie theater.

00:03:08.921 --> 00:03:10.122
I remember that.

00:03:10.122 --> 00:03:12.324
-The kid actors are like,
"Ha ha ha. Payphone."

00:03:12.324 --> 00:03:13.725
You're like,
"That's not that old!"

00:03:13.725 --> 00:03:15.194
-Exactly. [ Laughing ]

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:17.930
In fact, the kid had
a little trouble with the phone.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:19.365
There's a scene where the kid
gets on the phone

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:21.500
and he was a little like,
"How do I do this?"

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.002
And I was like,
"You've got to be kidding me."

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:24.170
-You've got to be kidding me.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:25.738
Well, he's a wonderful actor.

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:27.973
It's a really great,
really great cast.

00:03:27.973 --> 00:03:32.278
And Da'Vine was here yesterday.
-Da'Vine, yes.

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:33.846
-Your wonderful costar.

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:35.080
She was talking
about Alexander Payne,

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.515
who directed you in "Sideways."

00:03:36.515 --> 00:03:38.585
He does not have a place
for the actors

00:03:38.585 --> 00:03:41.253
to go watch themselves
between takes.

00:03:41.253 --> 00:03:42.655
He basically just stands
at the camera,

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:44.257
and you don't get to see
your performance

00:03:44.257 --> 00:03:45.724
until it's done.

00:03:45.724 --> 00:03:47.326
-Which is unusual.
-Do you like it?

00:03:47.326 --> 00:03:48.961
-I do. I like it much better.

00:03:48.961 --> 00:03:50.163
-Yeah?
-Because it kind of,

00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:52.965
it makes it like,
two different workspaces.

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:54.967
It's like, everybody is over
here doing the grunt work,

00:03:54.967 --> 00:03:56.736
the actors,
and everybody is over here

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:59.305
having their choco-chinos,
and they're sitting at the thing

00:03:59.305 --> 00:04:03.008
and going, "His hair looks
weird, his hair looks funny."

00:04:03.008 --> 00:04:04.310
All this kind of [bleep].

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.045
It's just like,
it eliminates all that [bleep].

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:07.280
-A good thing to eliminate.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:08.547
-It's a really good thing
to eliminate.

00:04:08.547 --> 00:04:10.283
And then he talks to you.

00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:11.984
He's off-camera
and he talks to you,

00:04:11.984 --> 00:04:14.454
and it's very relaxed
and intimate-feeling.

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:18.824
-It's a very relaxed and
intimate-feeling movie, as well.

00:04:18.824 --> 00:04:21.560
We were talking backstage,
it feels as much like a movie

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:22.761
that was made in the '70s

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:24.330
than that takes place
in the '70s.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.598
-Indeed, and that was the idea,
yeah, yeah.

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:27.066
-There's a really
wonderful scene --

00:04:27.066 --> 00:04:28.701
because I remember
the first time

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:32.438
I saw one of my teachers run.

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:34.440
And you play a teacher
and you're running,

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.376
you're chasing a student
down the hallway.

00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:40.913
And it's so shocking, the first
time you see your teacher,

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:42.881
like, chasing something,
because it reminds you --

00:04:42.881 --> 00:04:44.583
you're like,
"Oh, they are human beings."

00:04:44.583 --> 00:04:45.918
-They are human beings.

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:47.420
-And they're bad at running.
-Yes.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.023
And this guy's really not...

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:51.523
-How much thought did you
put into the run?

00:04:51.523 --> 00:04:52.891
Because it's a really good run.

00:04:52.891 --> 00:04:54.159
I didn't have to
think about it very much.

00:04:54.159 --> 00:04:55.194
-That's how you run?

00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:56.295
-Yeah, you know.

00:04:56.295 --> 00:04:57.730
That's kind of how I run.
-Okay, alright.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:58.897
-I take it all back.
It's a great run.

00:04:58.897 --> 00:05:01.834
-The knees are going,
everything.

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:03.969
-You know,
it was a swimmer's run.

00:05:03.969 --> 00:05:07.740
I can tell you swam
in your youth.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.675
-Thank you. Thank you very much.

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:11.176
-Your track coach
watched you run once

00:05:11.176 --> 00:05:13.946
and was like, "We're going
to get you in the pool, Paul."

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:16.649
-I tried to be a wrestler,
and that did not work at all.

00:05:16.649 --> 00:05:18.785
-Well, that actually, you might
have found something worse

00:05:18.785 --> 00:05:20.819
than swimming, though.
-Worse, much worse.

00:05:20.819 --> 00:05:22.455
Because then
you have to lose the weight.

00:05:22.455 --> 00:05:24.791
Wearing the garbage bags
to sweat.

00:05:24.791 --> 00:05:26.659
-Spitting in cups.
-Spitting in cups.

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:29.795
-Your ears are always
[bleep] up.

00:05:29.795 --> 00:05:31.597
-Horrible, horrible, horrible.

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:34.300
-And nobody wants to go.
-No, who cares?

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:36.269
No, nobody wants to go see
swimming, for Christ's sake.

00:05:36.269 --> 00:05:37.736
-Swimming is the worst.
-Swimming is the worst.

00:05:37.736 --> 00:05:39.439
Wrestling, at least somebody
might get hurt.

00:05:39.439 --> 00:05:40.506
Somebody might, you know --

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:41.941
The worst thing
that's gonna happen,

00:05:41.941 --> 00:05:43.342
you're going to whack your
fingers on the edge of the pool

00:05:43.342 --> 00:05:44.743
and be like, "Ahh."

00:05:44.743 --> 00:05:46.846
-But wrestling, it's bad.

00:05:46.846 --> 00:05:48.981
-I think the problem
when I was growing up, like,

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:50.350
because then wrestling
was all of a sudden,

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:51.884
it was like,
world wrestling, right?

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:53.419
And then when you go watch
high school wrestling,

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:55.854
you're like,
"This really stinks."

00:05:55.854 --> 00:05:57.589
-Nobody's doing anything!

00:05:57.589 --> 00:05:59.092
-There aren't even, like,
ropes to jump off.

00:05:59.092 --> 00:06:02.227
-Nothing, and everybody's kind
of still for a really long time,

00:06:02.227 --> 00:06:03.296
and nothing's really happening.

00:06:03.296 --> 00:06:04.563
-Like, "I feel
like this is holding.

00:06:04.563 --> 00:06:06.164
I'm watching holding."

00:06:06.164 --> 00:06:09.234
Talking about swimming,
I went and saw Olympic swimming,

00:06:09.234 --> 00:06:11.536
so I watched the highest level
of swimming in London.

00:06:11.536 --> 00:06:12.638
-Boring.
-Boring.

00:06:12.638 --> 00:06:14.840
Now, great on TV,

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:18.344
because again, the only good
vantage point is underwater.

00:06:18.344 --> 00:06:19.879
-Oh, that's true.

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:22.848
-Because otherwise, you're just
watching, like, seven splashes.

00:06:22.848 --> 00:06:25.083
-You're just watching this,
really slow.

00:06:25.083 --> 00:06:26.185
Like this.

00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:27.786
-"Oh, I liked that splash."

00:06:27.786 --> 00:06:31.023
-And also, the pool,
the atmosphere of a pool sucks.

00:06:31.023 --> 00:06:32.357
It's like, it's kind of hot.

00:06:32.357 --> 00:06:34.760
It's loud and weird.
It smells like chlorine.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.028
-Yeah, no.
-No, horrible.

00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:38.063
-It's like, when you walk
into a baseball stadium,

00:06:38.063 --> 00:06:39.765
you're like, "Oh, I love
the smell of fresh-cut grass."

00:06:39.765 --> 00:06:40.899
-Yeah, totally.

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:44.636
-But no one's
ever like, "Ah, the pool."

00:06:44.636 --> 00:06:48.341
-"Ah, the smell of
the wet bathing suit."

00:06:48.341 --> 00:06:50.809
-It is pointed out
by the character in the film,

00:06:50.809 --> 00:06:54.747
the kid, that your character
has a lazy eye.

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:57.049
-Yes. I can't remember
what the actual word for it.

00:06:57.049 --> 00:06:58.817
-Oh, yeah, I guess it's not.

00:06:58.817 --> 00:07:00.119
But my question is,

00:07:00.119 --> 00:07:01.754
was this an effect?

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:03.822
Was this a technical?
Are you not allowed to say?

00:07:03.822 --> 00:07:06.058
-I'm not at liberty
to discuss it.

00:07:06.058 --> 00:07:08.528
I'd have to kill everybody
in here if I talked about it.

00:07:08.528 --> 00:07:09.795
-It's a very interesting --

00:07:09.795 --> 00:07:12.597
-I'm capable of it.
I'm capable of it.

00:07:12.597 --> 00:07:15.134
-Nobody here doubts that.

00:07:15.134 --> 00:07:18.037
-I'll try. I'll chase everybody.

00:07:18.037 --> 00:07:20.273
It'll be really terrifying.

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:21.574
-This is a weird case
where I feel like

00:07:21.574 --> 00:07:24.377
we'd have to give you
a head start.

00:07:24.377 --> 00:07:27.579
[ Laughter ]

00:07:27.579 --> 00:07:29.114
-That is weird.

00:07:29.114 --> 00:07:33.486
It was done in a very --

00:07:33.486 --> 00:07:35.988
-It's very subtle and good,
and we weren't sure,

00:07:35.988 --> 00:07:37.590
but then we looked,
and so I just --

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.925
I want to ask,
there's a contact lens tech.

00:07:39.925 --> 00:07:41.293
-Yep.
-I've never seen that

00:07:41.293 --> 00:07:43.228
in the credits of a movie.
-Really? Because it happens.

00:07:43.228 --> 00:07:45.331
It's not an unusual thing.

00:07:45.331 --> 00:07:47.266
-And I'm assuming Zach Ripps
is one of the best.

00:07:47.266 --> 00:07:49.802
-Zach Ripps is --

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:52.438
No [bleep], Jack --

00:07:52.438 --> 00:07:55.541
Zach Ripps is top drawer.
He's the best at his job.

00:07:55.541 --> 00:07:57.676
-If you've got Zach Rips
on your contact lens,

00:07:57.676 --> 00:07:59.011
you've got a movie.

00:07:59.011 --> 00:08:01.347
-Pop in and out.
-That's how you got a movie.

00:08:01.347 --> 00:08:04.417
Well, let me tell you,
we're going to talk about,

00:08:04.417 --> 00:08:05.952
we are going to talk about
your podcast when we come back.

00:08:05.952 --> 00:08:07.887
But I really,
I can't stress enough,

00:08:07.887 --> 00:08:09.287
I think this is
a wonderful holiday film

00:08:09.287 --> 00:08:11.657
to watch with family,
and I can't recommend enough.

00:08:11.657 --> 00:08:12.691
And we're going to be
right back with more

00:08:12.691 --> 00:08:14.193
with Paul after this.

